%[text] # Constraint and Mapping Example
%[text] This example shows 3 things:
%[text] 1. Mapping input variables to a new input space because relationship from x to y is so nonlinear that fitting a lookup table is difficult. Large nonlinear changes are not well captured by regularizeNd or regularizeNdMatrices.
%[text] 2. Using a semilog space of the output, `log10(y)`. When the output changes so rapidly that it jumps several orders of magnitude, you may want to consider fitting `log10` of the output, `y`. The other reason is that the input to the output may be polynomial like. If it is polynomial like, a polynomial in log space is smoother and changes slower.
%[text] 3. Inequality and bounded constraints. Handling constraints are easy using regularizeNdMatrices with `lsqlin` or `lsqConstrainedAlternative`. Monotonic constraints and bounded constraints are easily handled in this example. Equality constraints are possible as well but are not discussed here. \
%[text] ## Load and Clean the Data
%[text] For what we are going to do, we need to discard $y\\le 0${"editStyle":"visual"}.
clc; clear; close all;
load('a06_constraints_and_mapping.mat','x','y');
index = y<=0;
x(index)=[];
y(index)=[];
%[text] ## Examine the Data
%[text] `x` to `y` is highly nonlinear. Specifically note the sudden and large changes in `y` at `x=6` and `x=6.45`.
screenSize = get(groot,'ScreenSize');
figurePosition = screenSize(3:4)*0.1;
% figureSize = screenSize(3:4)*0.8;
figureSize = [800 600];
figurePositionAndSize = [figurePosition figureSize];
figure('Position',figurePositionAndSize) %[output:2ad11e0d]
scatter(x,y) %[output:2ad11e0d]
xlabel('x') %[output:2ad11e0d]
ylabel('y') %[output:2ad11e0d]
set(gca,'yscale','log') %[output:2ad11e0d]
grid on; %[output:2ad11e0d]
title('Examing Data') %[output:2ad11e0d]
%[text] ## Map x to a New Space for a Smoother Map
%[text] The mapping is created manually and best explained via the video below.
%[text] ![](text:video:9656)
%[text] 
%[text] The idea is to make all sections of the data the same slope, $\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x}${"editStyle":"visual"}, or at least a similar slope. See the video on how I get a rough idea of the sections of the data. `pchip` interpolation is used because it is the smoothest interpolant that guarantees monotonic interpolation (aka no oscillations) in the mapping; a 1 to 1 mapping (1 x point maps to only 1 xNew point) is desired/required. The `akima` interpolation method does not always give a monotonic interpolation.
load('a06_constraints_and_mapping.mat','xValues','yValues')
desiredSlope = 1; % arbitrarily chosen
xDifference = diff(xValues);

% calculate the semilog slope
semilogSlope = diff(log10(yValues))./xDifference;

% scale slope in each section to the desired slope
xMapping = nan(size(xValues));
xMapping(1) = min(x);
for iX = 2:length(xMapping)
    xMapping(iX) = xMapping(iX-1) + semilogSlope(iX-1)./desiredSlope.*xDifference(iX-1);
end

mapping = griddedInterpolant(xValues, xMapping,'pchip','linear');

figure('Position',figurePositionAndSize) %[output:3ba4ef45]
fplot(@(x) mapping(x),[min(x), max(x)]) %[output:3ba4ef45]
xlabel('x') %[output:3ba4ef45]
ylabel('xMapped') %[output:3ba4ef45]
title('Monotonic, 1 to 1 Mapping of x to xMapped') %[output:3ba4ef45]

% plot new input to output
xMapped = mapping(x);
figure('Position',figurePositionAndSize) %[output:15ff9dcd]
scatter(xMapped, y) %[output:15ff9dcd]
xlabel('xMapped') %[output:15ff9dcd]
ylabel('y') %[output:15ff9dcd]
set(gca,'yscale','log') %[output:15ff9dcd]
grid on; %[output:15ff9dcd]
title('xMapped to y. Note how much smoother this is.') %[output:15ff9dcd]
%[text] ## Setup Fidelity and Regularization Matrices
%[text] 1. Setup an `xGrid` and then map it to the new `xMapped` space. The fitting will be done in the mapped space. 
%[text] 2. `y` is converted to `log10` space because `log10(y)` is smoother than `y`.
%[text] 3. Call regularizeNdMatrices. Get the fidelity matrix, `Cfidelity,` and the regularization matrix, `Lregularization` (in other cases, `Lregularization is "`matrices" if there is more than one input).
%[text] 4. Combine the fidelity matrix, `Cfidelity,` and the regularization matrix, `Lregularization,` into one composite matrix, `C`.
%[text] 5. Create the corresponding vector to `C, d.` \
%[text] Note that the matrices correspond to the objective function:
%[text] $J =\\frac{1}{2}||C\*yGrid-d||\_2^2$
%[text]  
xGrid = {linspace(min(x),max(x),1000)};
xGridMapped = {mapping(xGrid{:})};
yLog10 = log10(y);
smoothness = 5e-4;

[Cfidelity, Lregularization] = regularizeNdMatrices(xMapped, xGridMapped, smoothness);
C = vertcat(Cfidelity,Lregularization{:}); % note this line of code works even if Lregularization has more than one cell

dFidelity = yLog10;
dRegularization = zeros(size(C,1)-size(Cfidelity,1),1);
d = vertcat(dFidelity, dRegularization);

%[text] ## Setup the Constraint Matrix
%[text] In this case, the desired constraint is that the lookup table, `yGrid`, is monotonically increasing. Also, the bound is that no value in the table is less than 0.
%[text] The constraints are formed as inequalities like this:
%[text] $A\*y\_{Grid}\\leq b$
    % Monotonic constraint matrix
    [Amonotonic,bMonotonic] = monotonicConstraint(xGrid);
    
    nGridPoints = length(xGrid{1});
    
    % Bound constraint matrix
    Abound = sparse(nGridPoints,nGridPoints);
    Abound = spdiags(-ones(nGridPoints,1),0,Abound);
    
    % bound constraint vector
    bBound = sparse(size(Abound,1),1);
    
    % Composite constraint matrix and vector
    A = vertcat(Amonotonic, Abound);
    b = vertcat(bMonotonic, bBound);
%[text] ## Solve for the lookup table Using `lsqConstrainedAlternative`
%[text] Using the provided solver `lsqConstrainedAlternative` to find the lookup table `yGrid1`.
%[text] Create a function for use in evaluation.
    tic;
    yLog10Grid1 = lsqConstrainedAlternative(C,d,A,b);
    toc; %[output:4dc209f3]
    yLog10GridFunction1 = griddedInterpolant(xGridMapped, yLog10Grid1,'linear');
    yFunction1 = @(x) 10.^yLog10GridFunction1(mapping(x));
%[text] ## Solve for  lookup table Using `lsqlin`
%[text] Using the `lsqlin` solver has advantages. It has some more flexibility too but Optimization Toolbox is required`. lsqlin` can handle equality constraint but it is not shown here. In some cases `lsqlin` won't converge when `lsqConstrainedAlternative` does.
    % make sure lsqlin exists. If it doesn't warn the user they don't have the optimization toolbox
    if license('test', 'optimization_toolbox') %[output:group:26c53e4f]
        tic;
        options = optimoptions('lsqlin', 'Display', 'iter-detailed');
        yLog10Grid2 = lsqlin(C,d,A,b,[],[],[],[],[],options);
        toc;
        yLog10GridFunction2 = griddedInterpolant(xGridMapped, yLog10Grid2,'linear');
        yFunction2 = @(x) 10.^yLog10GridFunction2(mapping(x));
    else
        warning('Optimization Toolbox is not installed. Use lsqConstrainedAlternative or another provided linear solver.') %[output:272081bc]

        yFunction2 = @(x) nan(size(x));
    end %[output:group:26c53e4f]
%[text] ## Plot the Results
%[text] The fitted lookup tables work great in both the `lsqConstrainedAlternative` and `lsqlin` cases. 
%[text] Note that both fits are monotonically increasing and greater than 0.
figure('Position',figurePositionAndSize) %[output:19b9eaf2]
scatter(x,y,'b'); %[output:19b9eaf2]
hold on; %[output:19b9eaf2]
fplot(yFunction1,xlim,'r','linewidth',5); %[output:19b9eaf2]
fplot(yFunction2,xlim,'k','linewidth',3); %[output:19b9eaf2]
xlabel('x') %[output:19b9eaf2]
ylabel('y') %[output:19b9eaf2]
set(gca,'yscale','log') %[output:19b9eaf2]
grid on; %[output:19b9eaf2]
legend({'Scattered Data','Fitted Lookup Table from lsqConstrainedAlternative', 'Fitted Lookup Table from lsqlin'},'location','best') %[output:19b9eaf2]
